[Comments]
Count=13
1=Weekly study plot pit. Comp tests were both uneven showing unconsolidated nature of the facets above &
2=below the crust. The Nov. rain crust is variable in its structure, as it breaks down. There is some minor DH
3=development below the crust, but not a significant portion of the pack.
4=The surface hoar, now buried under 2cm of fresh snow is remarkable. We had SH as large as 8.5 cm at the plot
5=during this past cold snap - one might say they were of biblical proportions! The SH was limited by the tree
6=cover and disappeared at & above treeline. SH at the station was only as large as 4-5 cm - a function of the
7=warmer weather in the PR area.
8=We did a quicky pit on the south side of Parkers at tree line (6900') and found SH buried at 2cm down. Size
9=was 1-2 cm. There were small intermittent patches of the sun crust that Randy observed last week on the
10=southside, but not of any significant distribution. Our test on the south side was CTM6 on the rain crust in a
11=96cm snowpack.
12=Skiing on the south side is still great, and ski pen. is 10-20cm. The summer trail area is shredded up and
13=variable.
Stability=G

